1
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:08,806
The accelerometer, U1, measures how your head tilts and moves, in three directions.

2
00:00:08,806 --> 00:00:15,710
Its readings go into the 6 aux bytes of each packet, so software can spot movement that might spoil the EEG.

3
00:00:16,933 --> 00:00:27,942
It shares the SPI wires, but this chip can also speak another protocol, I²C, and busy SPI traffic could accidentally look like an I²C start signal to it.

4
00:00:27,942 --> 00:00:33,266
So U2, a tiny OR gate, holds its data pin high whenever it isn't selected.

5
00:00:33,266 --> 00:00:39,453
It's like an earplug that's only removed when the microcontroller wants to talk to it.

6
00:00:40,667 --> 00:00:47,961
The micro SD card slot, on the underside, lets the board record without a computer at all.

7
00:00:47,961 --> 00:00:52,662
It uses the same SPI wires, with its own chip-select, CS3.

8
00:00:52,662 --> 00:00:59,147
C4, a big 100 µF capacitor, covers the card's bursts of current while it writes.

9
00:01:00,333 --> 00:01:01,380
Chapter recap.

10
00:01:01,380 --> 00:01:07,210
The accelerometer adds motion to every packet, with an OR gate as its earplug.

11
00:01:07,210 --> 00:01:10,873
And the SD card lets the board record on its own.
